              {
                "text": "**Analyzing Pixel Distributions**\n\nI've been examining the behavior of the output grid based on two input pixel values and their positions. The initial example reveals a pattern where specific rows are filled uniformly with the input values. I see some rows filled with an entire row of values, whereas other rows are not uniform in their values. The next step is to formalize these observations and determine a general rule for output grid population, but I am still not clear on what determines which rows get filled how.\n\n\n**Deciphering Row Patterns**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I'm now certain there's an alternating pattern related to the row index! Looking at the rows that are completely filled in with a single pixel's value, I see that these are at even indices. This pattern holds across both examples, even when they're different sizes. The rows with values at the borders are at odd indices. I'm now working to formalize this alternation and figure out which pixel's value gets chosen.\n\n\n**Investigating Row Parity**\n\nI've been examining why the parity of the row index seems linked to whether a row is entirely filled with a single pixel value. I've noted that rows 0 and 1 are uniform for pixel 3 at (1,1) in example 1.  Rows 0 and 2 are filled with a uniform value in Example 2 as well, but now I'm attempting to connect the row index with this concept of distance from a uniform row, I suspect there's a pattern, particularly focusing on the role of the row's distance from the pixel's coordinate.\n\n\n**Identifying Edge Cases**\n\nI've realized something critical about the edge rows: they are always filled entirely with a single pixel's value. I noticed this across several examples. The top and bottom rows always exhibit this behavior. It appears that the edge of the grid has unique behaviour.\n\n\n\n**Discovering Full Row Conditions**\n\nI've made a crucial observation. Rows entirely filled with a single value are either edge rows (top/bottom) or contain a pixel. All other rows are only filled at their borders. This pattern holds consistently across both examples. I am beginning to see how a row is determined by whether the row has a pixel value, or is an edge!\n\n\n",
